  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}+I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1+1 & 0+0 \\\\ 0+0 & -1+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
